===Etymology===
[[w:List of pseudo-French words adapted to English|Pseudo-Gallicism]], from {{der|en|fr|double||double}} + {{m|fr|entendre||to mean, to [[understand]]}}. (The phrase is unused and ungrammatical in French.)

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|/dubl ɑ̃tɑ̃ːdɹ/|lang=en}}<ref name="OED">“[http://dictionary.oed.com/cgi/entry/50068990 ‖double entendre]” listed in the ''{{w|Oxford English Dictionary}}'' [2<sup>nd</sup> Ed.; 1989]</ref>
* {{a|UK|Anglicised}} {{IPA|/dʌbəl ɒnˈtɒndɹə/|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-us-double entendre.ogg|Audio (US)|lang=en}}
* {{audio|en-au-double entendre.ogg|Audio (AU)|lang=en}}

===Noun===
{{examples-right|examples=A woman walks into a bar and asks the barman for a '''double entendre''', so he gives it to her.}}
{{en-noun|double entendres|double entendre|pl2qual=nonstandard}}

# {{lb|en|idiomatic}} A [[phrase]] that has two [[meaning]]s, especially where one is [[innocent]] and [[literal]], the other [[risqué]], [[bawdy]], or [[ironic]]; an [[innuendo]].
#* '''1812''', ''A treatise on politeness'', tr. from the French by a lady, page 172
#*: Avoid all equivocal expressions, usually denominated '''double entendre'''; they are certain proofs of a mean and indelicate mind.
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=1891|author=Paulist Fathers|title=Catholic World|page=785|passage=It is a momentous crusade without the cross; and an insidious one, for the calumnies and '''double entendre''' against the church are well wrapped up and keenly distributed.}}
#* {{quote-text|lang=en|year=2000|author=James P. Lantolf|title=Sociocultural Theory and Second Language Learning|page=126|passage=It is not only the teacher's play with single words, phrases, and '''double entendre''' that are common in my classroom data.}}

